---
import StarlightPage from '@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro';
import UnreleasedNotice from '../../../components/reference/UnreleasedNotice.astro';
import { loadExport } from '../../../data/export';

const { attackReports } = loadExport();
---

<StarlightPage
	frontmatter={{
		title: 'Attack reporting',
		description:
			'Which addresses a FoxAuth server reports to FoxTrust each hour, why, every field of a report, and how to turn it off.'
	}}
>
	<UnreleasedNotice />
	<p>
		Every server on the internet is attacked. Someone guesses passwords at the sign-in page, tries stolen
		client secrets at the token endpoint, or asks for files like <code>/.env</code> that would hold
		secrets on a careless server. A FoxAuth server already refuses these requests. Attack reporting also
		tells FoxTrust which addresses did it.
	</p>
	<p>
		FoxTrust combines the reports of many FoxAuth servers. An address that guesses passwords at five
		unrelated servers in one hour is an attacker, and every server can know that before the address
		reaches it.
	</p>

	<h2>Why this is allowed</h2>
	<p>
		An IP address is personal data. Processing the addresses that attack a service, to protect that
		service and others, is a legitimate interest in network and information security (GDPR, Recital 49).
		No consent is needed for it, but you, the operator, must be able to say what is sent. This page is
		that statement: you can point your own privacy notice at it.
	</p>

	<h2>Turning it off</h2>
	<p>
		In the console: <strong>Settings → Integrations → Attack reporting</strong>. The change applies at once,
		without a restart, and what was counted and not yet sent is thrown away. This setting is the only
		switch. It is separate from usage reporting, so you can keep one and turn off the other.
	</p>
	<p>
		Turn it off while you test your own server, for example with a penetration test or a conformance
		suite. Otherwise your testing address is reported like any other.
	</p>
	<p>
		The card under the switch shows the exact report the server last sent, addresses included, and the
		tables below.
	</p>

	<h2>When a report is sent</h2>
	<p>
		Each server process counts attacks in memory, for one UTC hour at a time. In the first half of the
		next hour it sends one report, but only if some address crossed a threshold. A quiet hour sends
		nothing. A report that cannot be delivered is thrown away and never sent again, and it is not an
		error: the server shows nothing and logs nothing.
	</p>

	<h2>Where it goes</h2>
	<p>
		Reports go to <code>{attackReports.destination}</code>, run by the FoxAuth maintainers.{' '}
		{attackReports.retention} FoxTrust never lists an address because of one server's reports alone,
		because a report could be forged or come from a server whose proxy setting is wrong.
	</p>

	<h2>What counts as an attack</h2>
	<p>
		An address is reported for a type only once it reaches the threshold within one hour, in one server
		process. The thresholds are set so that an honest visitor who mistypes a password or asks for a second
		reset email never reaches them.
	</p>
	<table>
		<thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Reported at</th><th>What counts</th><th>What does not</th></tr></thead>
		<tbody>
			{attackReports.types.map((type) => (
				<tr>
					<td><code>{type.name}</code></td>
					<td>{type.threshold}</td>
					<td>{type.meaning}</td>
					<td>{type.notCounted}</td>
				</tr>
			))}
		</tbody>
	</table>

	<h2>Probe paths</h2>
	<p>
		A request for one of these paths is reported the first time. Each is a file or panel of another
		product that no FoxAuth server has, so only a scanner asks for it. A path ending in … covers
		everything under it. Other missing pages, such as a missing favicon, are never reported.
	</p>
	<table>
		<thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>Paths</th></tr></thead>
		<tbody>
			{attackReports.probes.map((probe) => (
				<tr>
					<td><code>{probe.label}</code></td>
					<td>
						{probe.paths.map((path, index) => (
							<>
								{index > 0 ? ', ' : ''}
								<code>{path}</code>
							</>
						))}
					</td>
				</tr>
			))}
		</tbody>
	</table>

	<h2>Which addresses are never sent</h2>
	<p>
		Only public internet addresses leave the server. Private, loopback, link-local, carrier-grade NAT,
		documentation, benchmarking, multicast and reserved addresses are never sent, nor anything that is
		not an address. An IPv6 address is sent only as its /64 network, never in full. Which address a
		request came from follows your <a href="/docs/deploy/reverse-proxy/">caller address setting</a>, so
		set it to match how the server is reached.
	</p>

	<h2>Every field</h2>
	<p>
		This is format {attackReports.format}. A report carries at most {attackReports.maxAddresses}
		addresses, those with the highest counts. Nothing else is in it: no account, password, code, client
		identifier, token, project, path, query, header, user agent or body.
	</p>
	<table>
		<thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What it says</th><th>Values</th></tr></thead>
		<tbody>
			{attackReports.fields.map((field) => (
				<tr>
					<td><code>{field.path}</code></td>
					<td>
						{field.meaning}
						{field.absentWhen ? <> Left out when {field.absentWhen}.</> : ''}
					</td>
					<td>{field.values}</td>
				</tr>
			))}
		</tbody>
	</table>
</StarlightPage>
